{
  "schema_version": 1,
  "face": "Create a photorealistic headshot of a beautiful woman, framed from the shoulders up.\n\n<image1> = REFERENCE FACE\n\nPreserve exact facial identity and natural hair.\nExclude glasses only if requested. Keep existing shoulder coverage; crop the composition to the head and shoulders.\n\nUse:\nsoft studio lighting\nshallow depth of field\nclean, uncluttered background\nrealistic facial proportions\nhigh-detail eyes, lips, and skin\nnatural color tones\nprofessional portrait photography look\n\nKeep the composition tight on the face only, with no full body visible.\nStyle should be realistic, refined, and high-end, like a beauty or fashion portrait.",
  "face_negative": "**Negative prompt:**\n\ndeformed face, distorted facial features, facial asymmetry, incorrect anatomy, unnatural proportions, malformed eyes, crossed eyes, misaligned eyes, uneven pupils, extra eyes, malformed mouth, distorted lips, bad teeth, duplicate features, warped ears, unnatural jawline, elongated neck, plastic skin, waxy skin, overly smooth skin, excessive skin retouching, artificial skin texture, excessive makeup, oversaturated skin, unnatural skin color, greasy skin, blurry face, soft facial details, low detail, low resolution, noise, compression artifacts, oversharpening, halos, harsh lighting, blown highlights, crushed shadows, flat lighting, unnatural shadows, messy hair artifacts, fused hair, floating hair, duplicate hair strands, cropped forehead, cropped chin, full body, half body, hands, visible hands, multiple people, duplicate person, text, watermark, logo, frame, border, illustration, painting, anime, cartoon, CGI, 3D render, doll-like appearance, uncanny face",
  "wardrobe": "Use WARDROBE SOURCE as the only outfit reference.\n\nCreate a **photorealistic wardrobe reference sheet** that breaks down the clothing and wearable accessories from WARDROBE SOURCE. Show **attire only**, **not the person**. No face, no skin, no visible body. Present the outfit on an **invisible mannequin / hollow garment display** with realistic shape and volume.\n\nExtract and preserve from WARDROBE SOURCE:\n\n* all clothing pieces\n* overall silhouette\n* layering\n* colors\n* fabric materials and textures\n* seams, stitching, buttons, zippers, buckles\n* belts, straps, harnesses, holsters, scarves, hats, gloves, footwear, and wearable accessories\n* wrinkles, weathering, dust, fading, scuffs, damage, and other visible wear\n\nIf some parts are hidden, reconstruct them logically and consistently from the visible design.\n\nUse a **flexible multi-panel layout**. Include several full-outfit views such as **front, back, and side** where useful, plus **additional zoomed detail panels** for important parts like collar, sleeves, closures, straps, pockets, belt area, footwear, fabric texture, and unique signature features.\n\nStyle: **professional costume department wardrobe board**, clean layout, neutral studio background, soft shadows, realistic materials, sharp detail, production-ready presentation.\n\nImportant: preserve the outfit faithfully, do not redesign it, do not turn it into a character sheet, do not show a human model, and keep the result **photorealistic and detailed**.\n\nVIEW CONSISTENCY:\nThe panels are different views of ONE outfit, not extra physical garments. Keep a consistent inventory in every full-outfit view. One hat, one of each single garment, one matching left/right footwear pair when present. Detail panels depict those same items. Do not add loose duplicate items or alternate designs.\n\nCOMPLETION:\nIf the source is cropped, follow the written outfit specification for missing regions. Otherwise design only the coordinated lower-body clothing and footwear needed for a complete outfit. Preserve an explicitly requested minimal outfit without adding unrelated layers. Establish these choices here and repeat the same choices in the front and back views.",
  "wardrobe_negative": "human model, person, character, face, head, skin, visible body, visible anatomy, hands, arms, legs, mannequin body, mannequin face, mannequin skin, mannequin head, mannequin limbs, posed character, character sheet, portrait, facial features, body reference, exposed flesh, floating human parts, redesign, altered costume, different outfit, unrequested wardrobe alternatives, missing clothing pieces, incorrect colors, changed materials, simplified details, missing accessories, extra accessories, incorrect layering, incorrect straps, incorrect belt, incorrect footwear, inconsistent outfit between panels, mismatched front and back, asymmetrical details not present in reference, duplicated accessories, fused garments, malformed clothing, impossible folds, broken seams, warped buttons, distorted buckles, incorrect closures, floating straps, disconnected straps, clipping, intersecting garments, melted fabric, plastic-looking fabric, fake texture, excessive wrinkles, excessive weathering, clean pristine clothing when reference is worn, fantasy embellishments, decorative additions, logos not present in reference, text, labels, captions, measurements, watermark, border clutter, busy background, environmental scene, cinematic scene, dramatic lighting, harsh shadows, illustration, concept art, sketch, painting, anime, cartoon, CGI, obvious 3D render, low resolution, blurry details, soft focus, noise, compression artifacts",
  "mannequin": "Create a clean three-panel image of one adult grey mannequin, reconstructing the visible body proportions of the supplied physique references. Change the depiction and surface material while preserving the source build. Render the mannequin views only, with no text, labels, diagrams or printed instructions.\n\nWhen supplied, FRONT BODY controls the visible torso and limbs: preserve chest/breast width, height, projection and placement relative to the ribcage, shoulders and waist; also preserve visible shoulder breadth, torso length, limb thickness and muscle definition. When supplied, REAR BODY controls visible pelvis width, buttock width and projection relative to the waist and thighs, back contour and rear musculature. These are relative shapes, not invented measurements or named body types. Match the supplied relationships equally when they are smaller or larger, narrower or wider, softer or more muscular. Carry the same body into all three views, accounting for view direction and perspective rather than copying a camera distortion.\n\nA photograph, drawing or stylized illustration is equally a shape reference. Converting it to a grey mannequin must not normalize its visible proportions to an average, idealized or retail-display body. Preserve clearly depicted body contours beneath close-fitting fabric while removing garment surfaces, seams and decorations. Loose cloth, armor, padding and accessories do not define underlying anatomy. Use only visible evidence; do not claim to recover hidden anatomy. A cropped upper-body source controls the visible upper body, not invented hips or legs; a cropped rear source controls the visible rear regions. Complete unspecified regions consistently with the established body and any explicit written details. Written physique requests change only the attributes they name. Gender/presentation does not prescribe measurements. Without body references, use the written physique specification. With neither, generate an adult mannequin without a prescribed body type.",
  "mannequin_negative": "text, letters, words, copied prompt, printed instructions, headings, captions, labels, annotations, typography, infographic, instruction poster, diagram, watermark, four panels, extra panel, extra back view, duplicate rear figure, two-panel layout, missing center figure, empty middle panel, omitted back view, extra figures, overlapping figures, duplicate front views, incorrect orientation, cropped full-body feet, cropped full-body head, tiny portrait, miniature bust, detached head, pedestal, bust stand, excessive empty space around portrait, clothes, underwear, fabric, garment seams, straps, hems, zippers, jewelry, hair, wig, eyebrows, eyelashes, realistic face, source identity, skin texture, grey-painted clothing, sculpted clothing, nipples, genital detail, reference silhouette drift, normalization of reference proportions, generic replacement physique, unrequested enlargement or reduction of body regions, invented muscle definition, unrequested slimming or bulking, clothing silhouette mistaken for body shape, mismatched front and rear physique, inconsistent portrait proportions, distorted proportions, extra limbs, white-on-white, overexposure, washed-out forms, missing form shadows",
  "sheet": "Retexture and dress the three existing figures in LAYOUT GUIDE. Preserve their body shapes and the three-panel composition. Make the surfaces photographic: natural human skin, the face and hair from FACE, and the clothing from WARDROBE.\n\nCopy the silhouette of each corresponding mannequin: shoulders, outer chest contour, waist indentation, hips, buttocks, thighs and calves. Keep the same chest-to-ribcage, chest-to-waist and hip-to-waist proportions, including deliberately pronounced or unusual proportions. Skin texture must not add body mass or soften away the waist. Keep the breast and glute volumes as large or small as the guide shows. The right portrait retains the scale, visible torso area and body volumes of the right mannequin, with the same physique as both full-body figures.\n\nFit the clothes around those existing volumes; resize the garments rather than the body. Copy their design, colors, material, straps, fastenings and accessories. Include matching footwear on both full-body figures when supplied. FACE supplies identity and hair only; WARDROBE supplies clothing only. If WARDROBE is absent, use the written outfit specification. Keep the guide's front view, rear view, upper-body portrait, framing and plain background. The finished image shows the original mannequin physique with realistic skin and clothes.",
  "sheet_negative": "body proportions different from LAYOUT GUIDE, chest or breast volume changed from LAYOUT GUIDE, hip width changed from LAYOUT GUIDE, glute volume changed from LAYOUT GUIDE, physique copied from FACE, wardrobe display physique, portrait body ratios inconsistent with full-body ratios, headshot framing replacing upper-body portrait, wardrobe reference board, costume breakdown sheet, garment-only board, copying wardrobe board composition, isolated garments, hollow garment display, clothing without a wearer, technical garment drawing, fabric swatches, zoomed garment insets, extra detail panels, headings, typography, diagram annotations, selected face absent, different person, multiple identities, identity drift, face drift, altered identity, generic face, different face between panels, different age, different skin tone, different hairstyle, different hair color, inconsistent hair, inconsistent body type, inconsistent proportions,\n\nmannequin appearance, mannequin face, mannequin head, mannequin joints, white mannequin material, plastic mannequin, doll-like body, featureless face,\n\nclothing from FACE, mixed outfits, wardrobe blending, alternate outfit, different outfit between panels, wardrobe drift, redesigned costume, restyled costume, simplified costume, recolored costume, invented garments, invented accessories, missing garments, missing accessories, extra accessories, incorrect layering, incorrect collar, incorrect sleeves, incorrect belt, incorrect straps, incorrect harness, incorrect jewelry, incorrect footwear, mismatched front and back wardrobe, unrelated rear design,\n\nblack clothing replacing colored clothing, dark red rendered as black, burgundy rendered as black, maroon rendered as black, brown rendered as black, charcoal rendered as black, inaccurate garment color, color shift, muddy colors, loss of material separation,\n\nunderexposed, dark exposure, low-key lighting, cinematic lighting, dramatic lighting, moody lighting, dim lighting, nighttime lighting, dark studio, dark background, black background, heavy shadows, harsh shadows, crushed blacks, black clipping, shadow clipping, excessive contrast, high contrast, dark color grading, muddy shadows, murky clothing, silhouette clothing, loss of shadow detail, hidden fabric texture, obscured garment details, obscured seams, obscured straps, obscured buckles, vignette,\n\nwrong layout, changed composition, extra panels, missing panels, two panels, four panels, merged panels, overlapping panels, reordered panels, changed panel proportions,\n\ncropped full body, cropped feet, missing footwear, incorrect scale,\n\nfront view in center panel, duplicate front view, three front views, side view in center panel, three-quarter center view, incorrect back view, face visible in back view, head turned toward camera in back view,\n\nfull body in right panel, small portrait, small face, distant portrait, side-profile portrait, three-quarter portrait, cropped face,\n\ndeformed face, facial distortion, facial asymmetry, malformed eyes, crossed eyes, bad anatomy, malformed hands, extra fingers, missing fingers, extra limbs, duplicate limbs, fused limbs, distorted feet, broken anatomy,\n\nfloating straps, disconnected straps, fused garments, clothing clipping, malformed clothing, impossible folds, melted fabric, plastic-looking fabric, fake leather,\n\nillustration, concept art, character art, anime, cartoon, comic, painting, sketch, stylized rendering, fantasy redesign, game character, CGI, 3D render, beauty filter, waxy skin, plastic skin, excessive skin smoothing,\n\nblurry, low resolution, soft details, motion blur, noise, compression artifacts, oversharpening, text, captions, labels, annotations, watermark, logo",
  "dataset": "TASK — ONE CHARACTER IN A NEW SHOT:\nGenerate one photograph of the approved character. FRONT CHARACTER is the assembled front-view reference for the complete outfit and physique. FACE is always supplied separately for identity, hair and skin, including when a rear reference is present. REAR CHARACTER, if supplied, is the same assembled character seen from behind: use it only for compatible details visible in the requested shot.\n\nPreserve this one identity, the approved body proportions, garment construction, colors, materials, layers and accessories. Clothing fits the body without slimming or changing chest/waist/hip ratios. Ignore reference backgrounds, fixed stances and camera framing. The shot instructions below control the current view, pose, distance and expression. A rear reference does not request a rear-facing output. Keep the face visible for frontal/three-quarter views; keep direct rear views facing away when explicitly requested.\n\nUse only these cropped individual references, never recreate their original three-panel sheet. One person, one image; no panels, inset portrait, mannequin material or loose wardrobe objects. Preserve natural skin and fabric detail, readable dark materials, coherent hands and feet, and no readable text.",
  "dataset_negative": "identity drift, different outfit, body proportion drift, mannequin material, duplicate person, contact sheet, panels, collage, inset portrait, rear view replacing requested front view, missing garments, mixed outfits, duplicated accessories, extra limbs, distorted hands, unreadable clothing detail, text, watermark",
  "mannequin_layout": "Use exactly three equal-width vertical panels across the landscape canvas. Left: one full-body front view, head to feet. Center: one full-body back view of the same mannequin, head to feet, facing directly away. Right: one enlarged frontal upper-body view of that same mannequin, from the top of the head through the entire chest to the lower ribcage. Keep chest-to-ribcage and shoulder-to-torso relationships identical to the left figure after accounting for scale. Exactly two full-body figures and one upper-body portrait; no additional views. Align the full-body figures at head and foot level, standing with arms slightly separated and feet slightly apart. OUTPUT CANVAS sets size only; an optional LAYOUT GUIDE sets framing, not physique.\n\nMake the surface smooth, opaque, matte medium-grey material with bald featureless heads. Remove source clothing, hair, jewelry, skin texture, nipples and genital detail without flattening or inflating the underlying body volumes. Use a pale neutral background and soft studio lighting with readable form shadows. Fill all three panels with their assigned views and return only the image.",
  "mannequin_preset": "Create a clean three-panel image of one adult grey mannequin from the selected physique attributes and written details. The supplied blank canvas sets output size only. Keep each selected attribute independent of gender and of other attributes; overall build does not override regional size or muscle definition. Unspecified attributes have no prescribed value. Use the same physique consistently in all three views. Render only the mannequin image, without text, labels, diagrams or printed instructions.",
  "mannequin_details": "Apply these physique details, refining or overriding only the attributes explicitly named while retaining all other selected attributes or reference proportions:",
